  {
    "index": 11,
    "hash": "fsnOzCAeWhgBRF6lsNakl/t+Vcc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T10:03:26.907Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Claim\n\nOutput-level benchmarks reward correct answers from broken processes the same as sound ones (Gettier applied to agents). Provenance makes reliabilist evaluation operational.\n\n## Process questions unlocked\n\n- **Skill contribution** \u2014 which skills correlate with correct outcomes vs contextslop\n- **Mechanism reliability** \u2014 inference vs retrieval-and-paraphrase vs guessing\n- **Failure localization** \u2014 which operation/skill introduced the error\n- **Counterfactual replay** \u2014 re-run the log with one skill removed\n\nThese are queries against a provenance graph, not new metric inventions.\n\n## Methodology grounding\nThe claims below live in the plugin's `data/methodology/` corpus; the relationship is stated per claim.\n\n- **[[agents are simultaneously methodology executors and subjects creating a unique trust asymmetry]]** \u2014 BUILDS_ON. Output scoring evaluates the executor role only. Process-level evaluation is what the subject role requires, and the methodology claim explains why the agent cannot supply it itself: it does not perceive the mechanisms constraining it.\n- **[[agent session boundaries create natural automation checkpoints that human-operated systems lack]]** \u2014 RELATES_TO. Both argue that the interesting evaluation happens at boundaries rather than in the middle of the work, where the actor is least able to observe itself.\n",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T10:03:26.907Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T10:03:26.907Z"
    }
  }
